WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.600
Authorization is the decision about what an authenticated identity is allowed to do.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:09.060
Authentication answers whether the subject is who or what it claims to be.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:19.840
Authorization answers whether that verified subject may read a record, update a file, call an API, approve a workflow, administer a tenant, or perform some other protected action.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:24.060
Successful login is only the beginning of access control, not the end.

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:29.880
It helps to separate identification, authentication, authorization, and accounting.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:34.100
Identification is the claim, such as a username or service identity.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.400
Authentication verifies the claim.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:40.460
Authorization determines permitted actions against protected resources.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:45.640
Accounting records activity for audit, investigation, billing, or operational review.

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:52.340
When teams blend these concepts together, they often create systems where being logged in is treated as permission to do too much.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:58.880
Subjects can be human users, service accounts, applications, workloads, devices, or API clients.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:08.060
Protected objects can be resources, records, files, functions, APIs, tenants, reports, administrative operations, or background jobs.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:14.760
The authorization question is always tied to a subject, an action, a resource, and relevant context.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:19.140
Without all of those pieces, the decision is usually vague or overbroad.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:25.520
Authorization failures can expose data, enable privilege escalation, or allow unintended system changes.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:33.500
They can also create operational problems when legitimate users cannot do their work or support teams do not understand why access was denied.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:42.760
Strong authorization is therefore a design and operations discipline: access should be predictable, appropriate, auditable, and aligned with business and security risk.
